    <h1>Bulk peptide sourcing risks buyers underestimate</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Price negotiation gets the attention; these are the risks that actually cost money at volume.</p>

    <h2>1. Single-supplier concentration</h2>
    <p>One supplier is efficient until it is not. A quality hold, a regulatory change or a capacity crunch leaves you without stock. Qualify a second source before you need it.</p>

    <h2>2. Lot-to-lot drift</h2>
    <p>A clean first lot does not guarantee the fifth. Ask for consecutive lot data and keep retention samples so you can compare.</p>

    <h2>3. Customs exposure</h2>
    <p>At volume, a single seizure is a large loss. Confirm classification, permits and destination rules before the first bulk shipment, not after.</p>

    <h2>4. Payment risk</h2>
    <p>Large prepayments to a new supplier are the highest-risk transaction in this trade. Use staged payments, escrow or documentary terms where possible. The instrument-by-instrument comparison, and the callback routine that prevents a spoofed bank-detail change, are in <a href="/knowledge/peptide-supplier-payment-terms/">supplier payment terms</a>.</p>

    <h2>5. Quality drift after qualification</h2>
    <p>Some suppliers perform during sampling and relax afterwards. Incoming testing on every lot is the only reliable control.</p>

    <h2>6. Documentation gaps at scale</h2>
    <p>Small orders get personal attention; large ones can slip. Specify the documentation package in the contract, not in email.</p>

    <h2>Mitigation checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Two qualified suppliers per critical SKU</li>
      <li>Written specification and acceptance criteria</li>
      <li>Incoming QC on every lot</li>
      <li>Retention samples for the shelf life</li>
      <li>Staged payment and clear Incoterms</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="ruo">Research use only.</div>


    <h2>A concentration-risk worked example</h2>
    <p>Suppose a distributor sources 80% of its tirzepatide volume from one supplier and holds four weeks of stock. A quality hold at that supplier — an OOS lot, a document discrepancy, a production pause — removes 80% of supply with four weeks of cover. At a typical reorder lead time of two to four weeks, the buffer is consumed before a new source can be qualified.</p>
    <p>The arithmetic is not the point; the asymmetry is. Qualifying a second source costs a sample order and a few weeks of record review. Losing the primary source costs the customer relationships you built on the first one. The second source is insurance, and like insurance it looks expensive until the day it is not.</p>

    <h2>What to put in the supplier agreement</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Specification annex:</strong> identity, purity, water, content and packaging, with acceptance criteria.</li>
      <li><strong>Change notification:</strong> the supplier must tell you before changing a process, a subcontractor or a specification.</li>
      <li><strong>Retention samples:</strong> defined retention period and storage conditions.</li>
      <li><strong>Remedy:</strong> replacement or credit for non-conforming lots, with a claim window and a defined evidence standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Business continuity:</strong> a named contact and an escalation path for supply interruptions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These clauses do not prevent a failure. They determine whether the failure is a conversation or a loss.</p>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>Volume amplifies whatever your supplier already is. A documentation gap that is tolerable at ten vials becomes a business interruption at ten thousand.</p>

